distinct-spatial-position-condition-b5dd.interpretation:
Physically, the condition $\mathbf{x} 
eq \mathbf{y}$ asserts that the spatial coordinates represented by the vectors $\mathbf{x}$ and $\mathbf{y}$ refer to two separate and distinguishable points in a spatial manifold, typically Euclidean $\mathbb{R}^3$. This distinction is a prerequisite for defining a non-zero distance vector, $\Delta\mathbf{r} = \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{y}$. In quantum field theory, this condition is critical for defining local causality (microcausality); for spacelike separated events, the commutator (for bosons) or anticommutator (for fermions) of field operators vanishes, e.g., $[\phi(\mathbf{x}), \phi(\mathbf{y})] = 0$ for $\mathbf{x} 
eq \mathbf{y}$ at equal times. This reflects the principle that measurements at spatially separated points cannot instantaneously influence each other. In many-body systems, this condition is implicit in interaction terms, such as the Coulomb potential between two electrons at positions $\mathbf{r}_i$ and $\mathbf{r}_j$ where the summation index $i 
eq j$ implies $\mathbf{r}_i 
eq \mathbf{r}_j$ to avoid a singularity.
